::GEO_U03_L06_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
An isosceles triangle has congruent sides AB and AC. Which angles are congruent?
{
=A. angle B and angle C#Base angles opposite the congruent sides are congruent.
~B. angle A and angle B#The vertex angle is not necessarily congruent to a base angle.
~C. angle A and angle C#The vertex angle is not necessarily congruent to a base angle.
~D. All angles must be 60°#That is true for equilateral triangles, not all isosceles triangles.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
An equilateral triangle has one angle measure of what?
{
~A. 45°#45° would not make three equal angles sum to 180°.
=B. 60°#Equilateral triangles are equiangular, and 180° divided by 3 is 60°.
~C. 90°#A triangle cannot have three 90° angles.
~D. 120°#Three 120° angles would exceed 180°.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
An isosceles triangle has vertex angle 40°. What is each base angle?
{
~A. 40°#The base angles are not necessarily equal to the vertex angle.
~B. 80°#Two 80° base angles plus 40° would total 200°.
=C. 70°#The base angles are equal and sum to 140°, so each is 70°.
~D. 140°#140° is the combined base-angle total.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L06_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
An isosceles triangle has base angles of 55°. What is the vertex angle?
{
~A. 55°#The vertex angle is not necessarily equal to each base angle.
~B. 110°#110° is the sum of the base angles.
~C. 125°#This does not make the total 180°.
=D. 70°#55 + 55 = 110, and 180 - 110 = 70.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L06_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which statement is true?
{
=A. Every equilateral triangle is isosceles.#Equilateral triangles have at least two congruent sides, so they are isosceles.
~B. Every isosceles triangle is equilateral.#Some isosceles triangles have only two congruent sides.
~C. No equilateral triangle is acute.#Equilateral triangles have three 60° acute angles.
~D. Every obtuse triangle is equilateral.#Obtuse triangles cannot be equilateral.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q06::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L06_Q06
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
If an equilateral triangle has side length 9, what are the other side lengths?
{
~A. 6 and 12#Those side lengths are not all equal.
=B. 9 and 9#All sides of an equilateral triangle are congruent.
~C. 9 and 18#Those side lengths are not all equal.
~D. Cannot be known#The equilateral classification gives enough side information.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q07::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L06_Q07
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
An isosceles triangle has congruent base angles. What can be concluded about opposite sides?
{
~A. All sides must be different.#Different sides contradict the isosceles relationship.
~B. The vertex angle is 90°.#No right angle is implied.
=C. The opposite sides are congruent.#Congruent base angles imply the opposite sides are congruent.
~D. The triangle must be scalene.#Scalene means no congruent sides.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q08::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L06_Q08
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
Which relationship supports solving an isosceles triangle angle problem?
{
~A. All triangles have four angles.#Triangles have three angles.
~B. Exterior angles are always 90°.#Exterior angle measures vary by diagram.
~C. All sides are different.#Isosceles triangles have at least two congruent sides.
=D. Base angles are congruent.#Isosceles triangles have congruent base angles.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q09::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L06_Q09
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
An equiangular triangle must also be what?
{
=A. Equilateral#A triangle with three congruent angles has three 60° angles and congruent sides.
~B. Scalene#Scalene has no congruent sides.
~C. Right#A right triangle has one 90° angle.
~D. Obtuse#An obtuse triangle has one angle greater than 90°.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q10::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L06_Q10
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
An isosceles triangle has base angle x° and vertex angle 80°. Which equation is correct?
{
~A. x + 80 = 180#This omits one base angle.
=B. x + x + 80 = 180#The two congruent base angles plus the vertex angle total 180°.
~C. 2x = 80#This incorrectly sets the base-angle sum equal to the vertex angle.
~D. x + x = 80#This ignores the full triangle angle sum.
}

